From 13fbc322052e5a75d884590c9f7e52c1160f6322 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 04:59:26 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] VideoPlayer: the text subtitle line on screen after a seek,
 found where the Matroska cues point

A seek of FFmpeg's demuxer lands on the video keyframe at or before the target and, in
Matroska, starts reading at the cluster of that keyframe (matroska_read_seek()): a text line
that started before that cluster is never read, and stays off screen until the next one. The
memory of SubtitleSeekMemory.h gives back the lines read before a seek; this finds the others
in the file, on a thread of its own, without reading what lies between.

What it reads (DVDDemuxers/MatroskaSubtitleSeek.h, pure):
- the file's index once, at the first seek: the EBML header, the SeekHead, the Info, every
  TrackEntry as stored, and the Cues (with what follows them up to 1 MiB, in one read).
  mkvmerge (by default: "iframes" for video and subtitle tracks) and FFmpeg's muxer write a cue
  for every subtitle block, with CueRelativePosition and CueDuration, which FFmpeg's demuxer
  ignores;
- the lines whose cue puts them on screen at the target, where their cue points: the head of
  their cluster and the block, a few hundred bytes. Without CueDuration, the cues that start in
  the window before the target, decided by their block's own duration; without
  CueRelativePosition, the cluster walked by the headers of its elements up to the block of the
  cue's time;
- without cues for the track: on this device and on the local network, the clusters from the
  window before the target walked by the headers of their elements, the bodies of the other
  tracks' blocks skipped; over the internet, where that is a request a block, the clusters just
  before the one the player reads from, in one range.
A block is taken only if it is of the track and, from a cue, at the cue's time. FFmpeg's stream
is matched to its track as matroska_parse_tracks() makes them (it sets no stream id in
Matroska). The packet is made by FFmpeg's own demuxer from a Matroska file of that one block
read from memory (MatroskaSubtitlePacket.h): the track's entry as stored (codec, private data,
content encodings), the cluster's timestamp, the block; its times as ConvertTimestamp() makes
them.

Measured on files made for the test (150 s at 37 Mbit/s, a keyframe every 5 s, TrueHD and AC-3,
SubRip, ASS and WebVTT tracks; muxed by FFmpeg, and by mkvmerge 97.0 as it is and without cues,
their positions or their durations, and with zlib), seeking as CDVDDemuxFFmpeg::SeekTime() does
to 144 times a track: FFmpeg's demuxer alone does not give 103 of the 203 lines on screen. With
the cues every one is found, in 1 to 4 reads of 4 KiB; without them the window is walked (30 s:
about 1700 reads, 7 MB, where reading it whole is 138 MB). Over HTTP byte ranges with 40 ms of
round trip, two requests a line: 127 ms a seek on average, 244 ms at worst; a walk there would be
200 requests and 8 s for 3 s of film, so it is not done. The packets are those FFmpeg gives
reading the file: data, times, duration and side data.

CSubtitleSeekSearch (SubtitleSeekSearch.cpp): a thread for each player, at the lowest CPU
priority and in the idle I/O class, with a reader of its own (a second handle of the file on
this device or the local network; over HTTP a curl session of that server, as for the
editions); a newer seek stops it, and it never holds anything the player waits for. A walk or a
range waits for playback to resume after the seek (4 s at most). Bounds by where the file is:
this device, 32 MiB, 4000 reads, a 30 s window, 2 s; the local network (SMB, NFS, HTTP to a local
address), 16 MiB, 1500 reads, 15 s, 3 s; the internet, 12 requests and 4 s for the cues, and
without them a range of at most what the player's cache read in a second before the seek (8 MiB),
taken at a quarter of that rate. A refusal of the server (429, 403...) ends the searches in that
file, never asked again.

The player takes what was found on its own thread (DeliverFoundSubtitles()): a line of the same
stream that has not ended goes to the subtitle player, which decodes a packet once
(CSubtitleSeekSeen, by its times and bytes) whether the search, the memory or the demuxer gives
it first. SubRip, text, ASS and WebVTT of the demuxer; not image subtitles.

kodi.log (INFO):
- "CSubtitleSeekSearch: <file> (<where>): N track(s), cues for M of them, K subtitle track(s)
  with cues; read in X ms (R read(s), B bytes)"
- "CSubtitleSeekSearch: after the seek to T s, N subtitle line(s) on screen there found in
  <file> (<where>), stored before where the demuxer reads again (by <way>; R read(s), B bytes,
  M ms)"
- "... the subtitle lines stored before where the demuxer reads again could not all be
  searched: <what>, <why> (...)", and lines for a file not searched and why (not on this device,
  the local network or HTTP; its index could not be read; no cues and no read rate over the
  internet; a stream that is not a track of the file; cues that do not point at their block)
- "CSubtitleSeekSearch: N subtitle line(s) found after the seek to T s had ended by the time
  they were found: not shown"

MatroskaHttpRange.h: Known() and Url(), for a source of a file whose length a first range of
another one gave. CApplication::Stop(): SUBTITLE_SEEK::StopSearches() after the readings of the
editions.
